#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "3312d0c4-51f9-5d97-b0e2-ad1261c39155" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100683 :question/id #uuid "622baf41-fe35-55ee-bfc6-f1e6a2cb089a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4371" :question/problem "Find the entry $a_{23}$ of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $a_{23}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $3$rd column. Therefore, $a_{23}={\\color{blue}{-3}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100989 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100684 :question/id #uuid "fe56a988-49e9-5f33-b0a5-f43d33984c1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4372" :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix ${B},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Square Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100990 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100685 :question/id #uuid "432a6a1a-ba9d-5aa0-b758-d4d32e9992f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4373" :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $C$ given below. Describe it in terms of $c_{ij}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 34 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry lies at the intersection of the $1$st row and $3$rd column. Therefore, we have $c_{13}={\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1167 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048129 :topic/math-academy-id 1673 :topic/title "Incidence Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252 :topic/math-academy-id 862 :topic/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048960 :topic/math-academy-id 4819 :topic/title "The Domain and Range of a Relation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048965 :topic/math-academy-id 233 :topic/title "The Minors of a 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980 :topic/math-academy-id 2083 :topic/title "Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120 :topic/math-academy-id 149 :topic/title "Elementary Row Operations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979 :topic/math-academy-id 146 :topic/title "Scalar Multiplication of Matrices"}] :topic/title "Index Notation for Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060 :topic/math-academy-id 742 :topic/title "Introduction to the Elimination Method"}] :topic/id #uuid "35070aa4-19de-5bfc-b9cc-08a2e69ce882" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192585 :question/id #uuid "c700cc22-203a-5ad3-913c-e5eb507e22e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13995" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${a + 4b = 1; 2a + b =-5$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n{(a + 4b = 1)\\cdot (-2); 2a + b =-5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2a - 8b &= -2 \\\\\n2a + b &= -5 \\\\\n(-8b + b) &= (-2 - 5) \\\\\n- 7b &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192586 :question/id #uuid "eb7f1f32-1a5c-526e-878b-8046c4feb8c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13997" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${3x + 4y = 2; x - 2y = 4$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(3x + 4y = 2; (x - 2y = 4)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 4y &= 2 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n(3x + 2x) &= (2 + 8) \\\\\n5x &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192587 :question/id #uuid "f20f46d9-b799-57a2-90bc-12e6593e8655" :question/math-academy-id "e-13999" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${2x + 3y = 8; x - 2y =-3$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(2x + 3y = 8; (x - 2y =-3) \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3y &= 8 \\\\\n- 2x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\n(3y + 4y) &= (8 + 6) \\\\\n7y &= 14 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= -3 \\\\\nx - 2(2) &= -3 \\\\\nx - 4 &= -3 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2$, which can be expressed as $(1,2)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192588 :question/id #uuid "990e3660-dfd7-50fa-b60b-0478ff02a073" :question/math-academy-id "e-14002" :question/problem "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${5u - 6v = 3; - 3u + 2v =-5$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3$, then the $v$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{5u - 6v = 3; (-3u + 2v =-5) \\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5u - 6v &= 3 \\\\\n- 9u + 6v &= -15 \\\\\n(5u - 9u) &= (3 - 15) \\\\\n- 4u &= -12 \\\\\nu &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3$. However, we still need to find $v$. To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 3(3) + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 9 + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n2v &= 4 \\\\\nv &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nu = 3, \\,v = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,2)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :topic/math-academy-id 4026 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048756 :topic/math-academy-id 6069 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Addition or Subtraction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048841 :topic/math-academy-id 847 :topic/title "The Remainder Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048982 :topic/math-academy-id 4236 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: Two Transformations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049777 :topic/math-academy-id 6413 :topic/title "Analyzing Linear Equations in General Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049946 :topic/math-academy-id 2007 :topic/title "Second-Order Homogeneous Recurrence Relations: Characteristic Equations with Distinct Real Roots"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/math-academy-id 1414 :topic/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245 :topic/math-academy-id 837 :topic/title "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/math-academy-id 5572 :topic/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions"}] :topic/id #uuid "38bc1722-543b-58cb-b448-fb93b0e623de" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073480 :question/id #uuid "f2105fec-c85b-5928-92e6-95ed80f1326c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14009" :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n${4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073481 :question/id #uuid "b1d34db3-7f30-5a8c-b233-fd955ac656f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14011" :question/problem "At how many points do the lines $x - 2y = 2$ and $10y - 5x = -10$ intersect?" :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 2; 10y - 5x =-10\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073482 :question/id #uuid "ab09c3eb-6813-57c6-891b-bf20196cbe93" :question/math-academy-id "e-14014" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $3x+2y=2$ and $6x+4y=4$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2; 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points"}] :topic/math-academy-id 493 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048343 :topic/math-academy-id 4638 :topic/title "Consistency and Dependency in Linear Systems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049754 :topic/math-academy-id 6295 :topic/title "Parametrizing Systems With Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050258 :topic/math-academy-id 6282 :topic/title "Larger Systems of Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050673 :topic/math-academy-id 6173 :topic/title "Determining Unknown Parameters in Systems of Linear Equations"}] :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049201 :topic/math-academy-id 3725 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049476 :topic/math-academy-id 3979 :topic/title "Parallel and Perpendicular Lines"}] :topic/id #uuid "3913d667-ede5-5fee-ba67-ff64afeb7883" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087686 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087433 :question/id #uuid "4f0593d8-bce5-5c73-aedd-ffe0faf15a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-14364" :question/problem "The lines $y=cx+7$ and $y=5x-3$ are parallel. What is the value of $c$?" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=cx+7$ is $c$.\n- The slope of the line $y=5x-3$ is $5$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $c=5$."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087434 :question/id #uuid "b2f31785-e173-518c-a1fd-b069e0834b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14367" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3$?\n\n1. $y=4x-8$\n2. $y=12x+1$\n3. $y=3x-12$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - y &= 3 \\\\\n- y &= -12x + 3 \\\\\ny &= 12x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is\n\n$$\ny = 12x+1\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087688 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087435 :question/id #uuid "88c5f90c-9bf9-58a2-a9a2-7c7ff1fc4f8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14370" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x$ and $kx-2y=-1$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=2x$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=-1$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n$kx - 2y|=-1; - 2y|=-kx - 1; 2y|= kx + 1; y|= \\frac{k}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{2}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{2}=2\n$$\n\nConsequently, $k=4$."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters"}] :topic/math-academy-id 837 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049930 :topic/math-academy-id 3556 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Parallel Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/math-academy-id 493 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050744 :topic/math-academy-id 6302 :topic/title "Linear Functions in Tabular Form Containing Unknown Parameters"}] :topic/title "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane"}]}
